.TH YHW.OLD 1 "November 2026" "exec" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
yhw.old \- interpret intent, refine prompts, and execute sudocode
.SH SYNOPSIS
.SY yhw.old
.RI [ options ]
.RI [ command ]
.RI [ "text | file | -" ]...
.YS
.SY yhw.old
.B exec
.RI [ "codex exec arguments" ]...
.YS
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B yhw.old
interprets requests in English or sudocode and uses Codex to carry them out.
Sudocode is precise, compressed, executable English shaped like short Unix
scripts.  Its commands may be invented when their meaning makes the intent
clear; pipes express how one result feeds another.
.PP
Arguments naming existing regular files contribute their contents.
Other arguments contribute literal text.
.B \-
reads standard input at that position.  Otherwise, piped input is appended
automatically, except in the two interactive shells.
With no arguments on a terminal,
.B yhw.old
starts
.BR nsh .
.PP
Unknown commands are interpreted as requests.  If no built-in mode was
selected and an executable named
.BI yhwh- COMMAND
exists in PATH, it receives the remaining arguments and inherited streams
instead.  A command name may end with a colon.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B gen
Print a rough draft prompt from a short request, then refine it through
conversation.
.TP
.BR ex ", " names ", " nm
Clarify an existing prompt.  Ask about one word or phrase per question,
offering roughly two to five choices and a free-form alternative.
The short forms
.B ex
and
.B nm
are quicker.  Announce when no questions remain.  When finished, print the
original prompt, a readable record of the answers, and an updated prompt.
.TP
.BR ask ", " "ex --test" ", " "names --test"
Switch roles: the user quizzes yhw.old about the intended meaning of a prompt.
.TP
.BR code ", " shell ", " sh
Rewrite a prompt as sudocode, preserving its information while making it
more precise and compact.  Sudocode may recursively invoke other invented
commands.
.B sh
uses fewer invented commands and options, at the cost of longer scripts.
.TP
.B nsh
Start an interactive sudocode shell.  Print
.B א.sh
in a startup banner, then
.B What?
and prompt with
.BR "~ @ " .
Interpret and carry out each line, retaining earlier definitions and results
in the conversation.
Aleph (א) is not an N; NSH is the nickname that caught on.
.TP
.B nhs
Start a Python-shaped sudocode shell with
.B ">>> "
and
.B "... "
prompts, snake art, and a Genesis 2–3 help menu. Startup quotes are from
We The Nameless, 01-genesis/02.tex (2:16) and 03.tex (3:9), with Hebrew
consonants rendered as Paleo-Hebrew using Phoenician Unicode letters.
A line ending in a colon, opening parenthesis, opening bracket, opening
brace, or backslash starts a block; a blank line submits it.
.B %paste
collects other multiline sudocode, and
.B :cancel
discards a block while it is being entered.
Earlier names, values, and intent remain part of the conversation.
This is an agent interface, not a Python interpreter.
.TP
.B add
Merge the supplied texts into one unified prompt containing all their
information.
.TP
.B do
Perform the requested work, preserving important invariants in the work
where appropriate.
.TP
.B ld
Read the supplied texts as a combined English executable, then become that
command.
.TP
.B exec
When used as the first argument, invoke
.B codex exec
directly with yhw.old's instructions and teaching examples.
Remaining arguments go to Codex without yhw.old's normal routing or input
assembly.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR -h ", " --help
Print usage and exit.
.TP
.BR -f ", " --force
Select full permissions, passing
.B --dangerously-bypass-approvals-and-sandbox
to the backend.
.TP
.BR -v ", " --verbose
Print the selected backend command to standard error.
Backend progress also streams to standard error.
.TP
.BI --effort " LEVEL"
Choose
.BR none ,
.BR low ,
.BR medium ,
.BR high ,
.BR xhigh ,
.BR max ,
or one of the thinking names below.
.TP
.BR -s " " MODE ", " --sandbox " " MODE
Choose
.BR read-only ,
.BR workspace-write ,
or
.BR full .
.TP
.BR --dry-run ", " @echo
Route the request and print the shell-escaped backend command without
executing the work.  Routing still invokes the backend unless both effort
and sandbox are specified.
.TP
.B --
Stop option parsing.  Remaining arguments are request input, still subject
to file and standard-input expansion.
.SH ROUTING
Unless explicitly selected, reasoning effort and permissions are chosen by
a separate read-only backend request.  Thinking harder alone does not
require broader permissions.
.PP
A leading thinking name also selects effort:
.B law
selects none;
.B lawd
selects low;
.B lord
selects medium;
.B lordy
selects high;
.B lordhavemercy
selects xhigh; and
.B tetragrammaton
selects max.
A thinking name can precede a command.
.SH INTERACTIVE USE
The prompt-drafting and clarification commands accept follow-up turns when
standard input is a terminal.  The two shell modes read turns from standard
input directly.  Conversation history is passed to subsequent backend
requests within that invocation; it is not a persistent interpreter process.
.PP
The host recognizes these commands between turns:
.TP
.BR :quit ", " :q ", " exit ", " quit
Leave the shell.  End of input also leaves.
.TP
.B :help
Print shell help, themed around the garden in nhs.
.B %help
and
.B ?
also show help in nhs.
.TP
.B :usage
Print CLI usage.
.TP
.BR exit() ", " quit()
Leave nhs.
.TP
.B :learn
Propose a good/bad teaching pair from the conversation.
.PP
Automatic teaching proposals occur every five successful shell turns by
default.  Only an affirmative terminal response appends the two commented
examples to the yhw.old script.  Proposals require a writable script and an
existing conversation.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I bin/yhw.old
The host script contains its backend configuration, thinking vocabulary,
teaching interval, instructions, and approved examples.  Paths here are
relative to the exec checkout; no separate profile file is required.
.SH EXIT STATUS
Zero indicates success.  Invalid wrapper options, missing option values,
invalid routing output, or an empty noninteractive request return 2.
Backend and delegated-command failures propagate their exit status;
a failed routing invocation returns 1.  An interactive shell can continue
after a failed turn.
.SH EXAMPLES
.EX
yhw.old gen: 'fix terminal focus in gui'
yhw.old names prompt.txt
yhw.old ex --test prompt.txt
printf '%s\en' 'Keep every requirement.' | yhw.old code
yhw.old add first.txt second.txt
yhw.old do 'fix the failing test'
yhw.old lordy do 'trace and repair the race condition'
yhw.old --effort low --sandbox read-only --dry-run code prompt.txt
yhw.old nsh
yhw.old nhs
yhw.old exec --help
.EE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR codex (1),
.BR lord.old (1)
